{"name":"Grey Heron","hex":"#89928a","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"grey heron","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Ardea cinerea, an Old World wading bird of the heron family."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/grey_heron"}],"entries":[{"title":"Grey heron","blocks":[{"text":"The grey heron is a long-legged wading bird of the heron family, Ardeidae, native throughout Europe, Asia, and most of Africa. It is resident in much of its range, but some populations from the more northern parts migrate southwards in autumn. A bird of wetland areas, it can be seen around lakes, rivers, ponds, marshes and on the sea coast. It feeds mostly on aquatic creatures which it catches after standing stationary beside or in the water, or stalking its prey through the shallows."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a9/Il_pescatore_solitario.jpg/330px-Il_pescatore_solitario.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grey_heron","wikidata":"Q25273"},{"title":"Heron","blocks":[{"text":"Herons are long-legged, long-necked, freshwater and coastal birds in the family Ardeidae, with 75 recognised species, some of which are referred to as egrets or bitterns rather than herons. Members of the genus Botaurus are referred to as bitterns, and, together with the zigzag heron, or zigzag bittern, in the monotypic genus Zebrilus, form a monophyletic group, Botaurinae, within the Ardeidae family. Egrets do not form a biologically distinct group from herons, and tend to be named differently because they are mainly white or have decorative plumes in breeding plumage. Herons, by evolutionary adaptation, have long beaks."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9e/Ardeidae-01.jpg/330px-Ardeidae-01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Heron","wikidata":"Q18789"},{"title":"Grey","blocks":[{"text":"Grey or gray is an intermediate color between black and white. It is a neutral or achromatic color, meaning that it has no chroma. It is the color of a rain or storm cloud, of ash, and of lead."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1e/Loxodonta_africana_by_Ron_Cogswell.jpg/330px-Loxodonta_africana_by_Ron_Cogswell.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grey","wikidata":"Q42519"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d9/ArdeaCinereaIUCN2019_2.png/500px-ArdeaCinereaIUCN2019_2.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d9/ArdeaCinereaIUCN2019_2.png/1280px-ArdeaCinereaIUCN2019_2.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d9/ArdeaCinereaIUCN2019_2.png/500px-ArdeaCinereaIUCN2019_2.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AArdeaCinereaIUCN2019_2.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d5/Grey_Heron._AMSM4086.jpg/500px-Grey_Heron._AMSM4086.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d5/Grey_Heron._AMSM4086.jpg/1280px-Grey_Heron._AMSM4086.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d5/Grey_Heron._AMSM4086.jpg/500px-Grey_Heron._AMSM4086.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A. c. cinerea at Jamnagar, India","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGrey_Heron._AMSM4086.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bc/Caudipteryx%2C_Senckenberg%2C_2017-10-12.jpg/500px